  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(2)$ but $-2\\ne 2.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,2],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
